# Firebase web config — the studio's only credential.
#
# Copy to .env.local and fill in from Firebase console → Project settings → Web
# app. These three are all the client needs: Google sign-in (auth) and nothing
# else. The API key is PUBLIC by design (it ships in the browser bundle);
# access is secured by Firebase Auth's authorized-domain list, not by secrecy.
#
# UNSET is a supported posture, not a broken one. With no project id,
# lib/firebase.ts reports firebaseReady = false, the landing page says auth is
# off, and every gated route fails CLOSED (bounce to /) rather than rendering
# the studio to everyone. Projects still live in the browser's IndexedDB — see
# lib/studioDb.ts — so a self-hosted single-user run needs no Firebase at all
# once you drop the gate.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=

# ── Local mode (lib/localMode.ts) ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The way to actually take the "needs no Firebase" posture above: set this to 1
# and the studio runs as a fixed local owner — no Google, no popup, any build.
# The data layer needs nothing extra: projects/steps/themes/assets already live
# in the browser's IndexedDB with zero dependencies; Firebase was only ever the
# doorman. Work is scoped to THIS browser profile and synced nowhere.
#
# Distinct from NEXT_PUBLIC_DEV_AUTH (a test-automation bypass, dead in
# production builds, amber banner). Local mode is a deployment posture and
# wears a quiet "local" badge instead. The money routes below still require
# their access secret either way — being local changes who you are, not what
# spending requires.
NEXT_PUBLIC_LOCAL_MODE=

# ── The reasoning engine (lib/text) ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Two routes reason over a built prompt — /api/recalibrate (notes -> an edit
# plan) and /api/frames (a script -> scene specs) — and both go through ONE
# chokepoint, lib/text/router.ts, which walks a fallback ladder:
#
#   rung 1  claude-cli   the machine's `claude` binary, billed to the LOGGED-IN
#                        SUBSCRIPTION. No API key exists for this rung — not
#                        here, not in a vault, not in a bundle. It is the app's
#                        default and needs no configuration at all.
#   rung 2  google       a metered Gemini key. The only rung that can answer on
#                        a managed platform, because Cloud Run has no binary and
#                        no interactive login.
#   (no floor)           there is no heuristic that writes an edit plan, so the
#                        bottom of the ladder is an honest refusal naming every
#                        engine that was tried and why each dropped out.
#
# WHICH ENGINE SERVED TRAVELS WITH THE ANSWER (`engine.rung`, `engine.transport`)
# and onto the version a recalibration stages. A cloud answer never renders
# indistinguishably from a local one.

# Which posture this deployment is in: `local` or `cloud`.
# Unset — the normal case — is decided by lib/deployment.ts from the platform
# itself: a managed serverless marker (K_SERVICE, VERCEL, …) means cloud, and
# anything else means local. Set it explicitly to REHEARSE the other posture:
# TEXT_ENV=cloud on a laptop is the only honest way to test the cloud path
# before deploying it. A garbage value falls through to the platform reading
# rather than defaulting to cloud — a typo must never silently start spending.
TEXT_ENV=

# Forbid (or force) spawning local binaries, independently of TEXT_ENV.
#   off  the machine could spawn `claude` and must not. Use it to stop the app
#        spending your Claude seat, or to rehearse the hosted posture.
#   on   override the managed-platform heuristic on a self-managed container
#        that genuinely carries the binary. It cannot conjure one — the probe
#        still has to pass.
# Unset = let lib/deployment.ts decide. Reported as `policy-forbidden` in the
# descent record, never as "binary missing": those send you to different fixes.
LOCAL_BINARIES=

# The cloud rung's credential. THE SAME KEY the imaging seam already uses — one
# Google credential, one auth model, both engines. Unset means the cloud rung is
# off, which on a local machine is fine and on Cloud Run means no engine at all.
# (Declared once, in the imaging block below. Named here because this seam reads
# it too, and a reader looking for the text engine's key should find the answer.)
#   GOOGLE_AI_API_KEY=  ← see the imaging block
#
# KEY FORMAT: AI Studio now issues keys in two shapes and BOTH WORK — the classic
# `AIza…` (39 chars) and the newer `AQ.…` (53). Verified 2026-08-27 against a live
# `AQ.` key: models.list and generateContent both answer 200. Either goes in the
# same variable and travels as the `x-goog-api-key` header. It is an API KEY, not
# an OAuth token: sending it as `Authorization: Bearer` earns a 401
# (API_KEY_SERVICE_BLOCKED), which is the expected result and not a broken key.
#
# Model per turn class, and these ids are a MEASUREMENT, not a guess — run
# `npm run verify:text -- --roster` to re-check them against the live roster
# before changing either. Defaults as of 2026-08-27:
#   edit-plan        gemini-3.1-pro-preview  (applied to a creator's manuscript
#                    and persisted, so it gets the capability floor; 15.5s)
#   scene-direction  gemini-3.6-flash        (reviewed frame by frame before
#                    anything is generated from it; 4.7s)
# `gemini-3.6-pro` does NOT exist on the roster — it was this table's first
# draft and every edit-plan turn would have 404'd. `gemini-pro-latest` works but
# is a floating alias and is deliberately not used: a turn whose cost is recorded
# has to name a model that means the same thing next week.
GOOGLE_TEXT_MODEL_PLAN=
GOOGLE_TEXT_MODEL=
# Only for pointing the adapter at a different base (a Vertex path, a proxy).
GOOGLE_TEXT_BASE_URL=

# ── Capability flags (lib/capabilities.ts) ──────────────────────────────────
#
# NOT a security boundary — hiding a control does not disable a route, and every
# money route stays gated by lib/apiAuth.ts and fails closed on its own. These
# exist so a deployment that CANNOT do something says so instead of offering a
# button that 503s after the user has composed something.
#
# All default to ON, so a normal checkout behaves exactly as it does today with
# none of these set. A HOSTED deployment turns off what it genuinely cannot do:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_CAP_MUSIC_SECTION_EDIT=0   section editing + raw compose. The
#     music vendor's stored-song inpainting has no Google Cloud equivalent —
#     a missing product surface, not a model difference. Nothing to adapt to.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_CAP_MUSIC_SFX=0            text-to-sound-effect. Same shape:
#     no GCloud service generates a discrete, exact-duration, loopable effect.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_CAP_LOCAL_VIDEO=0          clip rendering on the local GPU rig
#     (ComfyUI, pipeline/vlm-probe). Needs a GPU and the VRAM/RAM headroom
#     guards; Cloud Run with a GPU is a different product and a decision nobody
#     has taken. See docs/video-generation-plan.md.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_CAP_DESKTOP_TOOLING=0      anything assuming the app and the
#     operator share a machine. Structurally impossible when hosted.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_CAP_MUSIC_GENERATE=0       the Score phase's cue render. The
#     only PARTIALLY portable one: the cue->plan doctrine in lib/music/plan.ts
#     would survive a port to a GCloud music model; the wire format would not.
#     Leave it on until an adapter exists, or turn it off rather than let it
#     fail per click.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CAP_MUSIC_GENERATE=
NEXT_PUBLIC_CAP_MUSIC_SECTION_EDIT=
NEXT_PUBLIC_CAP_MUSIC_SFX=
NEXT_PUBLIC_CAP_LOCAL_VIDEO=
NEXT_PUBLIC_CAP_DESKTOP_TOOLING=

# ── Imaging vendors (lib/imaging) ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# UNLIKE the Firebase block above, these are SECRETS. None is prefixed
# NEXT_PUBLIC_, and none may ever be: they are read only inside route handlers
# under app/api/imaging/*, and each one bills a real balance. See docs/imaging.md.
#
# Which vendor answers is decided by IMAGING_ENV. A caller may steer within
# that table (`prefer`/`avoid` on any /api/imaging request — `avoid` is the move
# after a safety refusal), but it can never name a vendor this table does not
# plan for the capability:
#   dev   Google generates and edits · Qwen recognises · Leonardo is the
#         re-route target for generate, reached only if Google refuses
#   prod  Google does all three
# Unset it and the app follows NODE_ENV — dev locally, prod in a production build.
IMAGING_ENV=

# Leonardo — app.leonardo.ai/api-access. The dev re-route target for generation
# (Lucid Origin), not the dev default: it reads no style references, so a
# style-locked request never lands here.
# Note: every generation this app makes is DELETED after the image is
# downloaded, so the account's gallery stays clean. See lib/imaging/providers/leonardo.ts.
LEONARDO_API_KEY=

# Qwen / DashScope — recognition in dev (qwen3.8-max). International endpoint by
# default; set QWEN_BASE_URL to the Beijing host if the account lives there.
QWEN_API_KEY=
QWEN_BASE_URL=

# Google AI Studio — all three capabilities in production, and generate+edit in
# dev as well. Without this key, dev generation falls back to Leonardo and
# style-locked generation cannot run at all.
# Nano Banana 2 for generate/edit (NOT Lite — Lite has no style references),
# Gemini 3.6 Flash for recognition. GOOGLE_IMAGE_SIZE is 0.5K/1K/2K/4K.
# Both model ids are overridable below, for when the next one ships.
GOOGLE_AI_API_KEY=
GOOGLE_IMAGE_MODEL=
GOOGLE_VISION_MODEL=
GOOGLE_IMAGE_SIZE=

# ── Music vendor (lib/music) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# SECRET, server-only, read lazily per call like the imaging keys. Unset means
# the music engine is off, not broken: /api/music/generate answers 503 with a
# sentence that says exactly that. The Score phase renders cues without it.
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=

# ── Music spend ceiling (lib/music/budget.ts) ───────────────────────────────
#
# DENOMINATED IN SECONDS OF AUDIO, NOT DOLLARS, and that is the honest unit
# rather than a convenient one. ElevenLabs bills music against a CREDIT balance;
# no credits-per-second rate has ever been measured here, so lib/music/pricing.ts
# carries three deliberately UNPRICED rows rather than an invented conversion. A
# ceiling in dollars would have to invent that conversion to work at all, so the
# ceiling counts the one quantity this repo knows exactly: how many seconds of
# audio it asked for.
#
# It REFUSES rather than bills - assertWithinMusicBudget throws over-budget (402)
# before the vendor is touched. A timeout or a malformed response still books
# (the render ran); a refusal, a rate-limit or a missing key does not, so a 401
# loop cannot eat a ceiling it never spent.
#
# Unset = 600 seconds per 1-hour window. `0` means RENDER NOTHING - it is not a
# way to disable the ceiling, and there is no way to disable it.
MUSIC_BUDGET_SECONDS_PER_WINDOW=
# The rolling window, in ms. Unset = 3600000.
MUSIC_BUDGET_WINDOW_MS=

# ── Route access gate (lib/apiAuth.ts) ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# The money/compute routes are access-gated. They FAIL CLOSED: with
# IMAGING_ACCESS_SECRET unset, every one of them answers 401. Set it to any
# hard-to-guess string and present it as `Authorization: Bearer <secret>` (or
# `x-imaging-access-secret`).
#
# WHICH ROUTES — nineteen, not the four this block used to name. The old text
# said "app/api/imaging/{generate,edit,recognize} and app/api/frames", which was
# true when it was written and has been false since music, recalibrate and the
# foundry landed. A reader who trusted it concluded that music and foundry were
# ungated, which is the opposite of the truth. The list is derived, not retyped:
#
#     grep -rln "guardRequest\|guardAccessOnly" app/api/
#
#   · app/api/imaging/{generate,edit,recognize}   3
#   · app/api/frames                              1
#   · app/api/music/{generate,plan,compose,sfx}   4
#   · app/api/recalibrate                         1
#   · app/api/foundry/**                         10
#
# Run the grep rather than trusting this comment — a count in prose is a claim
# with a shelf life, and this one already expired once. `npm run preflight`
# reports the gate's live state (open/closed) without naming a single route.
#
# firebase-admin is not a dependency here, so this is a shared secret rather than
# a Firebase ID-token check. A browser can only present a secret that shipped in
# its bundle, so the client copy below is PUBLIC — this bounds casual abuse and
# rate, not a determined attacker. Wire firebase-admin to upgrade to real
# per-user identity; the server already accepts a Bearer token.
IMAGING_ACCESS_SECRET=
# The browser's copy of the same value (inlined into the bundle, hence public).
# Set it equal to IMAGING_ACCESS_SECRET so the studio's own UI can call the routes.
NEXT_PUBLIC_IMAGING_ACCESS_SECRET=
# In-memory per-IP token bucket. Defaults: 30 requests / 60s.
IMAGING_RATE_CAPACITY=
IMAGING_RATE_WINDOW_SEC=
# Ceiling on how many distinct client keys the limiter will HOLD. Default 10000.
#   Powers: the limiter's own memory bound. `x-forwarded-for` is caller-supplied,
#     so without a cap a spray of forged values grows the bucket map for the life
#     of the process.
#   Without it: nothing — the default is a real bound, not unlimited.
#   Too low: the limiter starts evicting buckets that still hold someone, which
#     returns allowance it had refused. `[api] rate PRESSURE` says so on the
#     server log, and `rateStats().counters.evictedPressure` counts it. A
#     non-zero pressure count means either this is set too low or somebody is
#     spraying the keyspace.
#   Trust class: server-side operational tuning. Never NEXT_PUBLIC_.
IMAGING_RATE_KEY_CAP=

# ── Spend ceiling (lib/imaging/budget.ts) ───────────────────────────────────
#
# The chokepoint (lib/imaging/router.ts) refuses a call that would push spend in
# a rolling window past this ceiling — priced with the pre-call estimate. Default
# is 5 USD per 1-hour window; a call over it throws `over-budget` (HTTP 402).
IMAGING_BUDGET_USD_PER_WINDOW=
IMAGING_BUDGET_WINDOW_MS=
